<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <!-- Review reminders. POST_NOTIFICATIONS is runtime-requested on Android 13+
         (see ReviewNotificationService.requestPermission), deliberately at the
         moment a review clock actually starts rather than on cold start.
         RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED lets pending reminders survive a reboot.
         SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM is intentionally NOT declared: Play restricts it,
         and a study reminder does not justify exact-alarm privileges. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS"/>
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED"/>

    <!-- Android Auto Backup is OFF, decided 2026-08-15 during the pre-Play
         security audit. Both attributes are needed: `allowBackup` covers API 30
         and below, `dataExtractionRules` covers 31+ (cloud backup *and*
         device-to-device transfer, which is a separate channel).

         `allowBackup` defaults to **true**, so this was on for the whole life of
         the app without anyone deciding it. What it did: uploaded the SQLite
         record (`app_flutter/sahm.sqlite`) and the preferences beside it to the
         student's Google Drive, and restored them onto their next phone.

         Why off. The first version of this reasoning kept it on, arguing that
         Ather's own cloud backup is opt-in so this was the only thing standing
         between a student who never enabled it and a lost phone. **That was
         wrong, and it is worth recording why rather than just deleting it.**
         `BackupSyncNotifier` uploads on a 20-second debounce after any write and
         again on every backgrounding, for every signed-in student, with no
         toggle anywhere — and the auth gate makes signing in mandatory. So every
         student already has an automatic Firestore copy, and
         `BackupSyncGate._restoreIfEmpty` pulls it onto a new phone unaided.

         Once that is fixed the durability argument is nearly empty: Auto Backup
         only runs on an **unmetered** network, so it never rescued the
         offline-only student it was supposed to protect either. Both paths need
         the same connection. What it did reliably provide was a second copy of a
         minor's exam history, in a place this app does not control and cannot
         delete — including on account deletion, where the best the deletion page
         can do is tell the student to go clear it themselves.

         The one real loss: a student whose compressed record passes
         `_maxBlobBytes` fails with `BackupFailure.tooLarge` and now has no cloud
         copy at all, where Auto Backup would have caught them. That is a reason
         to page or raise the ceiling — tracked as a follow-up — not a reason to
         keep an uncontrolled second copy.

         **If this is ever turned back on: the database and `shared_prefs` are one
         unit, and backing up one without the other is a data-mixing bug, not a
         privacy tightening.** §3.30's one-account-per-device wipe compares an
         incoming uid against `last_account_uid` in preferences. Restore the
         database without that preference and `previous` reads null, the wipe
         never fires, and the *second* student to sign in on a restored phone
         opens the *first* student's curriculum — exactly the failure §3.30 was
         written to fix. Verify any such change with a real backup → wipe →
         restore cycle on a device you are free to erase; the include-domain for
         `app_flutter/` is easy to get wrong and the mistake is silent. -->
    <application
        android:label="Ather"
        android:name="${applicationName}"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher">
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTop"
            android:taskAffinity=""
            android:theme="@style/LaunchTheme"
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|smallestScreenSize|locale|layoutDirection|fontScale|screenLayout|density|uiMode"
            android:hardwareAccelerated="true"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">
            <!-- Specifies an Android theme to apply to this Activity as soon as
                 the Android process has started. This theme is visible to the user
                 while the Flutter UI initializes. After that, this theme continues
                 to determine the Window background behind the Flutter UI. -->
            <meta-data
              android:name="io.flutter.embedding.android.NormalTheme"
              android:resource="@style/NormalTheme"
              />
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
        <!-- Load-bearing, and easy to miss: since flutter_local_notifications 16
             the plugin's own manifest declares **permissions only** — verified
             against the copy in the pub cache, which contains nothing but
             POST_NOTIFICATIONS and VIBRATE. The two receivers below are what
             actually turn a scheduled alarm into a notification, so without them
             `zonedSchedule` succeeds, the alarm fires, and nothing is ever shown.
             That is a silent failure with no error anywhere, which is exactly how
             it went unnoticed.

             ScheduledNotificationReceiver  -> shows a scheduled reminder.
             ScheduledNotificationBootReceiver -> re-arms pending reminders after
             a reboot or an app update, which is what RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED above
             is declared for. A 30-day cycle will cross at least one of those. -->
        <receiver
            android:exported="false"
            android:name="com.dexterous.flutterlocalnotifications.ScheduledNotificationReceiver" />
        <receiver
            android:exported="false"
            android:name="com.dexterous.flutterlocalnotifications.ScheduledNotificationBootReceiver">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.BOOT_COMPLETED"/>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MY_PACKAGE_REPLACED"/>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.QUICKBOOT_POWERON" />
                <action android:name="com.htc.intent.action.QUICKBOOT_POWERON"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>

        <!-- Don't delete the meta-data below.
             This is used by the Flutter tool to generate GeneratedPluginRegistrant.java -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="flutterEmbedding"
            android:value="2" />
    </application>
    <!-- Required to query activities that can process text, see:
         https://developer.android.com/training/package-visibility and
         https://developer.android.com/reference/android/content/Intent#ACTION_PROCESS_TEXT.

         In particular, this is used by the Flutter engine in io.flutter.plugin.text.ProcessTextPlugin. -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.PROCESS_TEXT"/>
            <data android:mimeType="text/plain"/>
        </intent>
    </queries>
</manifest>
